Now that {A} is even-times-even is clear, for it has not its half odd. [!just VII.Def.8]

I say next that it is also even-times-odd.

If we bisect {A}, then bisect its half, and do this continually, we shall come upon some odd number which measures {A} according to an even number. If not, we shall come upon a dyad, and {A} will be among those which are doubled from a dyad, which is contrary to the hypothesis.

Thus {A} is even-times odd.

But it was also proved even-times-even. Therefore {A} is both even-times-even and even-times-odd.

Therefore, *if an [even] number neither is one of those which is continually doubled from a dyad, nor has its half odd, then it is both even-times-even and even-times-odd.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Numbers which are both even-times-even and even-times-odd are are both divisible by 4 and an odd number. They are the numbers whose halves are even excluding powers of 2.

This completes the series of propositions on even and odd numbers that started with @IX.21.
